# b02_chain_has_list: Collections (Every method, "Lazy chains"). The page
# says "In an error message a chain is named like a list (`[Int]`), and its
# "has:" line is the list's without those four" (push, pop, insert,
# remove_at). Every example of the "has:" list calls the method on a list;
# this calls an unknown method on a chain.
# Meant to FAIL.
# EXPECTED OUTPUT:
# error: `[Int]` values have no method `frobnicate`
#   --> corpus/m120/b02_chain_has_list.lume:17:28
#    |
# 17 |   puts xs.map { |n| n * 2 }.frobnicate
#    |                            ^
#   help: `[Int]` has: len, empty?, any?, all?, first, last, max, min, sum, sort, sort_by, reverse, contains?, join, map, filter, reject, each, count, find, take, skip, take_while, fold, min_by, max_by, enumerate, to_list, zip, flatten, uniq, index_of, avg, group_by, partition, flat_map, to_set, at, to_s, to_str

def main:
  xs = [3, 1, 2]
  puts xs.map { |n| n * 2 }.frobnicate
